<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Einheitsfrontlied</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">December <a href="/years/1934">1934</a>; first performed <a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Einheitsfrontlied&quot; (United Front <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>) is one of the best-known songs of the German labour movement, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht">Bertolt Brecht</a> and set to music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song arose from the defeat of the German left after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> came to power in January 1933. The rivalry between the Social Democratic Party and the Communist Party had long divided it, and once the Nazis banned both parties and the labour unions in the summer of 1933, many, Brecht among them, believed that only a united front of social democrats and communists could resist fascism. In December 1934, at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> director Erwin Piscator, Brecht wrote the song as a call for all workers to join the Workers' United Front, the Arbeiter-Einheitsfront.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a>, who later composed the East German anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a>, deliberately kept the music simple so that workers without musical training could sing it, which gives it the character of a march.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Each verse begins from the needs that make a person human, food, clothing and freedom from oppression, and argues that talk and drums satisfy none of them. The refrain calls the comrade to fall in to the left and take his place in the workers' united front. The last verse holds that the liberation of the workers can only be the work of the workers themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the following year a choir of 3,000 workers performed the song at the First International Workers Music Olympiad in Strasbourg. Its first record, sung by the communist actor and singer Ernst Busch, appeared in 1937, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, and Busch's remains the best-known rendition. Brecht included the text in his 1939 collection Svendborger Gedichte, and in 1948 Eisler wrote a symphonic version. In 1943 Shmerke Kaczerginski based the text of his Partizaner-Marsh on it, for the Fareynikte Partizaner Organizatsye, a Jewish resistance group in the Vilna Ghetto. Ton Steine Scherben recorded it in 1971 and Hannes Wader in 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Einheitsfrontlied” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Einheitsfrontlied</b>&quot; (United Front Song) is one of the best-known songs of the German labour movement, written by Bertolt Brecht and set to music by Hanns Eisler.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li></ul></section>
